A||N||4||glava2876||84 2M4 questions||||5||
Z||000000||glava2876||04-11-2001||09:54 PM||||I just aquired an 84 2M4 -5 speed. Good news -37,000 original mile SE -loaded, even has sheepskin leather interior with speakers buit into the headrest. Bad news -it has been in storage for about 10 years with nothing done to it prior to being put away -other than jackstands and a cover. What are all of the steps that I should take before trying to start? It has records of a recall done for the disk brakes -where do I find info on other recalls? Thanks for any guidance anyone can provide. Thanks, Glen||||reg||5
Z||000001||Fierowrecker||04-11-2001||10:36 PM||||Hey Glen!<BR>Look at the exhaust manifold...<BR>If it is a cast iron unit, it needs a new manifold...<BR>If it is a tubular manifold, make sure it has the oil deflector installed...<P>Getting your car started should be easy...<BR>Pull the plugs, spray WD-40 into the cylinders (just a little!), check under the distributor cap for critters, change the oil and filter, and drain the fuel tank (if there is any!) and replace with fresh gas...<P>You may find the distributor full of rust, if so, you should change it...<BR>crash...<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Rodrv6||04-11-2001||10:54 PM||||Probably just a typo, but 84's didn't come with 5 speeds, only 4 speeds or automatics.<BR>Also, my 84 came with a tubular exhaust manifold from the factory.  I don't recall ever seeing a 4 cylinder in a Fiero with a cast iron exhaust manifold.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting, 88 GT, not nearly as many miles :)||||reg||1
Z||000003||84Bill||04-11-2001||11:09 PM||||That must mean 1 engine was replaced or 2 they change the manifold when they did the recall||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||04-11-2001||11:42 PM||||All fiero 4 cyl manifolds I am aware of are weld ups. (tube steel.) <P>the post recall one is the same as the 87-88. no heat stove. (the heat stove moved to the pipe going to the catalyst.)<P>Shoot some light oil, Preferably something heavier than WD40, in each cyl and let it soak overnight. About 1/4 to 1/2 ounce of marvel mystery oil would be a good candidate.<P>change the oil the next day. You don't want to pump any of that old crap around. god knows what's in the bottom of that pan.<P>crank the engine at least 2 turns with a wrech on the crank bolt. If you can't turn it with a big wrench the starter is only likely to break something. Make sure you turn the wrench in the normal direction the motor spins. (CCW???)<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 04-11-2001).]||||reg||1
